# Project 2 - Nondeterministic Finite Automaton (NFA)
Trevor Fry
Caleb Backer
CS 361 001 - Intro to Theory of Computation
Spring 2026

## Overview

This project implements a Nondeterministic Finite Automaton (NFA) in Java using the formal 5-tuple definition
(Q, Sigma, delta, q0, F). The NFA supports adding states and alphabet symbols, defining transitions including
epsilon transitions, marking start and final states, simulating input strings, computing epsilon closures, checking
whether the automaton behaves like a DFA, and tracking the maximum number of active NFA copies during execution.

The implementation follows the provided interfaces and is validated using the supplied JUnit test cases.

## Reflection

This project helped reinforce the differences between deterministic and nondeterministic automata by requiring us
to track sets of active states rather than a single current state. One of the more interesting parts was handling
epsilon transitions correctly, especially when computing epsilon closures and applying them during simulation.
Another important piece was using Java collections to model transition mappings and state sets in a way that stayed
clean and matched the theory from class.

Overall, this project strengthened our understanding of graph traversal, automaton simulation, and the connection
between formal models of computation and object-oriented program design.

### How to Compile and Run
This project uses JUnit 4 for testing.

#### Windows (PowerShell)
From the project root directory:

Compile:

```powershell
javac -cp ".;lib\junit-4.13.2.jar" ./test/nfa/NFATest.java
```

Run the tests:

```powershell
java -cp ".;lib/junit-4.13.2.jar;lib/hamcrest-core-1.3.jar" org.junit.runner.JUnitCore test.nfa.NFATest
```

#### macOS / Linux
From the project root directory:

Compile:

```bash
javac -cp .:/usr/share/java/junit.jar ./test/nfa/NFATest.java
```

Run the tests:

```bash
java -cp .:/usr/share/java/junit.jar:/usr/share/java/hamcrest/core.jar org.junit.runner.JUnitCore test.nfa.NFATest
```

